// EXPECT: E0255 — `#no_abc` on a `#foreign` procedure (ADR-0058 §3)
//
// `#no_abc` suppresses a procedure's bounds checks. A `#foreign` declaration has no body, so there
// is no index in it to leave unchecked — the directive could only ever be a word that does nothing.
//
// **Refused rather than ignored**, and the difference matters more than it looks. Someone writing
// this has asked for unchecked indexing across a boundary Jairs does not compile at all: the C
// function's own array accesses were never Jairs' to check. Silently accepting the directive would
// tell them their request was granted. Refusing it says the request has no meaning here, which is
// the true answer.
//
// This is the one interaction ADR-0058 §3 rules out. `#no_abc` on an ordinary procedure is legal
// (`tests/corpus/valid/047-no-abc.jr`), and `#no_abc` *with* `#c_call` is legal in either order —
// only the `#foreign` combination is refused, because only that one has no body.

/// The library, so the only diagnostic in this file is the one it is named for. A `#foreign`
/// declaration naming an undeclared library is E0225, and a refusal file with two unrelated errors
/// makes the second one look like a consequence of the first.
libc :: #system_library "c";

/// The refusal. `#no_abc` after the return type, then a `#foreign` binding with no body.
read_raw :: (fd: s64, buf: *u8, count: s64) -> s64 #no_abc #foreign libc "read";
